---
const meanings = [
  ["Possibility", "It might rain.", "Rain is possible. The speaker is unsure."],
  ["Expectation", "The bus should arrive soon.", "The speaker expects it, but does not guarantee it."],
  ["Strong prediction", "The bus will arrive at noon.", "The speaker presents this confidently."],
  ["Obligation", "You must wear a seat belt.", "It is required."],
  ["Advice", "You should take a coat.", "The speaker recommends it."],
  ["Permission", "You may leave.", "The speaker allows it."],
  ["Ability", "I can swim.", "The speaker is able to do it."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Modal Verbs - What they mean</h1>
  <p>
    A <strong>modal verb</strong> is a helping verb that comes before another verb and changes how we understand the action. The common ones are <strong>can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would</strong> and <strong>must</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>She <strong>might arrive</strong>. You <strong>must stop</strong>. I <strong>can swim</strong>.</p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-green">
    <h2>Read the meaning from the sentence</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">What it tells us</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          meanings.map(([meaning, example, tells]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{meaning}</th>
              <td>{example}</td>
              <td>{tells}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</div>
